he position  representation can be written  as the sum of a kinetic energy term  and a potential energy term  let's now consider the radial equation  of a particle moving  in three dimensions  this was the latest expression that we  got for the radial equation in terms of  the u function  this equation has the form of an  eigenvalue equation for a hamiltonian  which i will call the radial hamiltonian  it is given  by the term in square brackets  we can now combine these two terms to  define a new function  v sub f and we can then  rewrite this radial hamiltonian like  this  if we now compare this latest expression  for the radial hamiltonian  with the one up here for a particle  moving in one d  we see that our radial hamiltonian looks  like that of a particle of mass m  moving in a one-dimensional potential vf  for this reason we call it an effective  potential  and this is also the reason for the  subscript eff  here to make the analogy complete with  the 1d case above  we need to remember that the radial  variable r cannot be negative  so you can think of this effective  potential as having an infinite wall  for negative r right so this is all very  neat  but why do we do it using the concept of  an effective potential means that we can  use  everything we already know about  particles moving in one dimension  to solve this new problem this is a  strategy that we use in many different  areas of physics  if we can rewrite a new problem in a  mathematical form  that parallels the mathematical form of  another problem that we have already  solved  then we can solve our new problem much  more easily because we can use  all the prior knowledge about the  problem that we have already solved  this is true not only for the maths but  actually also for the physics  if we can connect seemingly different  problems in this way  then this typically provides new  connections between different areas of  physics that bring out  fundamental connections that may not  always be  superficially apparent  but going back to our effective  potential let's see what it all means  in this particular example of a particle  moving in a three-dimensional central  potential  our real potential is v of r here  this extra term in the effective  potential comes from the original  kinetic energy term what happened is  that the original problem was a  three-dimensional problem  and we've reduced it to this one  variable problem in terms of  r only and in doing so some terms have  acquired a form  that no longer resembles the usual form  of a kinetic energy and quantum  mechanics  when we think in terms of effective  potentials we therefore forget  that this is part of the kinetic energy  because it's more convenient to think of  it  as an effective potential energy  conceptually we're trying to describe a  three-dimensional problem  using an effective motion in one  dimension and this  extra term encodes the effects of the  angular part  of that motion so let's draw a pair of  factors  on the horizontal axis we place the r  variable  and on the vertical axis we first place  the real potential  let's now pick an example of a central  potential and we can use the attractive  coulomb potential which is proportional  to -1 over r  we can schematically draw it right here  let's now add the effective potential to  the vertical axis  looking at the definition up here we see  that we actually have  a different defective potential for  every value of l  for l equals zero the effective  potential is equal to the real potential  and we can just add it to the diagram  for any l larger than 0 we end up with  the sum of the real potential and then a  term that goes like 1 over r  squared as one over r  squared increases faster than one over r  decreases  then the effective potential looks  something like this  for l equals one for larger l  we have the same asymptotic behavior and  the only difference is that the  proportionality constant associated with  the 1  over r squared term becomes larger  so the effective potentials will deviate  more from the real potential  for example for l equals 2 we would get  something  like this this discussion is actually  something that you may have already  encountered  in classical mechanics where central  potentials can also be described  in terms of effective one-dimensional  potentials  for the radial part of the problem if  this were a classical potential  then for the real attractive coulomb  potential  v of r then a particle would feel an  attractive force towards the origin  everywhere however for any of the  effective potentials with  l larger than zero we actually see that  close to the origin the potential has a  negative slope  so the force that a classical particle  would feel becomes repulsive  in this situation the effective  potential is usually called a  centrifugal potential  another term typically used to describe  this part  of the effective potential is the  angular momentum barrier  if we remember that the radial function  r vanishes for  l larger than zero this now rationalizes  that result  by showing that this angular momentum  barrier  pushes the wave function away from the  origin  i've used an attractive coulomb  potential for this example  but we would find a similar behavior if  we had used a different  central potential so feel free to try it  out  on your own  overall the eigenfunctions of the  hamiltonian describing  a central potential can be written in  terms of a radial part  and an angular part the angular is given  by spherical  harmonics which simultaneously obey the  eigenvalue equations of the square  orbital angular momentum  and the lz component of orbital angular  momentum  we solved these in the videos on orbital  angular momentum  and they are exactly the same for any  central potential  the radial function r obeys this  differential equation  called the radial equation  unlike the angular part this radial part  changes depending on the form of the  rail potential here  and you can find a few examples in the  videos that we've linked  in the description to actually solve the  radial equation  it is convenient to rewrite the radial  function  r in terms of a new function u  and this function u obeys this simpler  differential equation  subject to adding this boundary  condition to ensure  that we get valid solutions  we will leave it here today but check  out our other videos for examples of  specific radial equations and their  solution  now that we know what the radial  equation looks like we're ready to study  a lot of different systems for example  you can check out our videos on the  three-dimensional isotropic harmonic  oscillator or on our favorite system the  hydrogen atom  and as always if you liked this video  please subscribe
 

